<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Santiago Mariño</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1788">1788</a>–<a href="/years/1854">1854</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> Mariño was a Venezuelan independence leader who in 1813 freed eastern Venezuela from Spanish rule while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a> campaigned in the west. He shared power uneasily with Bolívar in the Second Republic, which fell to royalist forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-tomas-boves">José Tomás Boves</a> in 1814. He later fought in the wars of independence and was active in Venezuelan politics after 1830.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/santiago-marino.jpg" alt="Santiago Mariño" width="320" height="463" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Martín Tovar y Tovar (1827 - 1902) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Martin_Tovar_y_Tovar_34.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
